WEBVTT
TITLE Contiguous Memory Allocation And Fit Policies — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Contiguous allocation gives a process one uninterrupted run of physical memory.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
That single assumption leaves two questions open: where the block goes, and what happens to the space left over.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The whole topic is one list of blocks and one subtraction per request.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Fixed partitioning: 1024 KB is cut into eight partitions of 128 KB before any process exists.

5
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Five processes are placed, and the unused part of each partition is wasted inside it: 18, 33, 0, 88 and 52 KB.

6
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.000
P6 asks for 130 KB and cannot run, although three partitions are free.

7
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
The account: 449 KB in use, 191 KB internal waste, 384 KB in free partitions, so 575 KB of 1024 is unusable.

8
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Variable partitioning: 2500 KB holding six holes of 200, 400, 600, 500, 300 and 250 KB.

9
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:13.500
A 357 KB request takes 357 of the 400 KB hole and leaves 43 KB as a hole.

10
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:22.000
When a process leaves, its block becomes a hole; two holes that touch merge, two that do not stay apart.

11
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:30.000
First fit: scan from address zero and take the first hole that is large enough. 357 goes into the 400 KB hole, leaving 43.

12
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
210 goes into the 600 KB hole, leaving 390; 468 goes into the 500 KB hole, leaving 32.

13
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The fourth request of 491 KB is refused, although 1215 KB is free and the largest hole is 390 KB.

14
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:54.000
First fit is fast because it usually stops early; its cost is that the low addresses are rescanned for every request.

15
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Best fit takes the smallest hole that is large enough: all four requests are placed and 724 KB is left in six holes.

16
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Worst fit takes the largest hole: two requests are placed and the third is refused with 1683 KB free.

17
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Best fit keeps the large holes for the large requests that arrive later, and pays for it by scanning every hole.

18
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Same six holes, same four requests, four placements against two — the policy is the only difference.

19
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Next fit is first fit with a rover: the scan resumes where the last allocation stopped.

20
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.000
On this stream it places three of four, the same count and the same layout as first fit.

21
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:46.000
A searched witness: with holes 100, 200 and 700 KB, first fit leaves 0 KB where next fit leaves 600 KB.

22
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.000
External fragmentation: the free bytes exist, in six pieces, and no single piece is large enough.

23
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The diagnostic is two numbers: total free space, and the largest single hole.

24
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
The average hole size is 202.5 KB, which is not the number that decides anything.

25
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
No placement policy prevents external fragmentation for arbitrary arrivals and departures.

26
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Internal waste sits inside an allocation the allocator has already given away; external waste sits in the free list.

27
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:31.000
Fixed partitioning creates internal waste by construction; variable partitioning creates external waste by history.

28
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Compaction cures the second and cannot touch the first; paging still has internal waste.

29
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Compaction moves the live blocks together, so 1215 KB of scattered free space becomes one hole of 1215 KB.

30
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:54.000
The bill is 1285 KB copied, in eight blocks — proportional to the live bytes moved, not to the number of holes.

31
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.000
It needs dynamic relocation: every address a process holds must be rewritable at run time.

32
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:08.000
And it must not move a block that a device is reading: pin it, or do I/O through kernel buffers.

33
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
A 1994 paper question: blocks of 50 in use, 150 free, 300 in use, 350 free, 600 in use; requests 300, 25, 125, 50.

34
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.000
First fit places all four: 350 gives 50, then 150 gives 125, then 125 exactly, then 50 exactly.

35
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Best fit places three: it serves 25 from the 50, leaving two 25 KB holes that cannot hold 50.

36
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:36.000
So "best" is a description of the immediate leftover, not a guarantee about the outcome.

37
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:43.500
First, fix what the question fixes: the policy, the request order, and whether compaction is allowed.

38
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:51.000
Then trace mechanically: keep address order, take requests in order, and write the remainder immediately.

39
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:58.500
Then the two diagnostics: internal or external, and a capacity shortage or a shape problem.

40
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:06.000
On this page's own stream: first fit 3 of 4, next fit 3, best fit 4, worst fit 2 — from identical holes.

